\DOC SIMD_SIMPLIFY_ABBREV_TAC

\TYPE {SIMD_SIMPLIFY_ABBREV_TAC : thm list -> thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Simplifies the most recently added assumption of the goal — a word-level
equation describing a SIMD register or memory value — leaving the conclusion
and all other assumptions unchanged.

\DESCRIBE
The two arguments are theorem lists: {unfold_defs}, definitional equations each
stating that a defined word operation equals its word-level expansion, and
{unfold_aux}, auxiliary rewrite equations relating those same defined
operations combined with word addition/subtraction to equivalent word-level
forms. Both are used to simplify the last assumption. The tactic normalises the
right-hand side of that equation, in particular collapsing nested
{word_join}/{word_subword} lane-extraction patterns down to the individual lane
terms. The rewritten assumption is placed at the end of the assumption list, so
the assumption order shifts even when the simplified form is syntactically
identical to the original.
{
         A, t ?- u
   =================  SIMD_SIMPLIFY_ABBREV_TAC unfold_defs unfold_aux
         A, t' ?- u
}
\noindent where {t'} is the simplification of {t} and may equal {t}.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single-subgoal, single-assumption-rewriting
behaviour, with uniformly large machine-state goals whose last assumption is an
equation reading a vector register or memory location.

\ENDDOC
